# Per-test knobs for ci/tasks/run-tests.sh. Sourced, so plain assignments only.
# EXPECT_IN_OUTPUT and FORBID_IN_OUTPUT hold one extended regex per line, all
# matched against the second run's build log.

# The task's own end-to-end check (all four dependency caches populated before
# the build started) plus the runtime's `[cache]` events, which name the tier
# each one actually came from. The events are what catch a tier that quietly
# turned itself off: rclone treats a copy from a key that does not exist as a
# success, so "Restoring ..." on its own proves only that an attempt was made.
EXPECT_IN_OUTPUT="S3 RESTORE VERIFIED
\[cache\] tier s3=enabled scope=setup-runtime-s3-cache-test
\[cache\] restore maven/repository from=s3 files=[1-9]
\[cache\] restore maven/wrapper-dists from=s3 files=[1-9]
\[cache\] restore gradle/modules-2 from=s3 files=[1-9]
\[cache\] restore gradle/wrapper-dists from=s3 files=[1-9]"

# This test has no `caches:` block, so its local tier is cold on every run and S3
# is the only thing that can have filled these. `from=local` appearing here means
# the test stopped testing what it is named after. There is no `from=none` to
# forbid - an empty cache logs nothing - which is what makes the EXPECT list
# above the real check: a tier that silently did nothing prints no line at all.
FORBID_IN_OUTPUT="\[cache\] tier s3=disabled
\[cache\] restore (maven|gradle)/[a-z0-9-]+ from=local
\[cache\] (restore|save) [^ ]+ .*status=failed"
